// Change: new rule
// Title: Linux Reverse Shell via Socat or Cryptcat
// Sigma ID: c3f7a1e8-5d2b-4f74-c6e3-4a8b2d7f1c50
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Ivan Saakov
// Date: 2026-05-10 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: execution, T1059.004
// Description: Detects execution of socat or cryptcat with arguments indicative of a reverse shell setup. Socat
//   is a powerful networking utility that can redirect standard I/O to a network socket, making it a
//   common tool for establishing reverse shells that bypass firewall rules. Cryptcat adds encryption
//   on top, evading plaintext content inspection. Unlike netcat, socat is less commonly flagged by
//   default endpoint controls.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate use of socat for authorized port forwarding or debugging
//   - Security researchers testing in sandboxed environments
// References:
//   - https://gtfobins.github.io/gtfobins/socat/
//   - https://www.revshells.com/
//   - https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/socat.1.html
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/e5bac28da3655c33390db7ef1503db8cd3babb5f/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_socat_reverse_shell.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "/socat" or FolderPath endswith "/cryptcat") and (ProcessCommandLine contains "EXEC:" or ProcessCommandLine contains "/bin/sh" or ProcessCommandLine contains "/bin/bash" or ProcessCommandLine contains "/bin/zsh" or ProcessCommandLine contains "pty")
